DESIGN LOGIC:
The system uses the Producer-Consumer pattern. One producer thread
reads images and enqueues tasks into a shared circular
buffer. N consumer threads dequeue tasks, perform matrix-vector
multiplication against pre-trained weights, and store predictions.

A mutex protects all queue access. Consumers wait on not_empty when
the queue is empty, and the producer signals it after each enqueue.
Termination is handled by pushing N poison pill tasks (is_last=1)
after all images are processed, each consumer exits when it
dequeues one. The mutex is released before computation so all
consumers can run their math in parallel.

Pseudocode:

Producer:
  open images.bin, labels.bin
  for each image:
    lock mutex
    while buffer full: wait on not_full
    enqueue task
    increment count, advance tail
    signal not_empty
    unlock mutex
  for each worker:
    lock mutex
    enqueue poison pill (is_last=1)
    signal not_empty
    unlock mutex
  close files

Consumer:
  loop forever:
    lock mutex
    while count == 0: wait on not_empty
    dequeue task, advance head, decrement count
    signal not_full
    unlock mutex
    if task.is_last: pthread_exit
    compute dot product (pixels x weights)
    find argmax of output
    store prediction in predictions[image_id]

PERFORMANCE SUMMARY:
Buffer Size: 10,000
| Threads | Accuracy        | Execution Time |
| 1       | 86.49% (8649/10000) | 0.2901s    |
| 4       | 86.49% (8649/10000) | 0.0861s    |
| 8       | 86.49% (8649/10000) | 0.1290s    |

PERFORMANCE ANALYSIS:
Accuracy was consistent across all thread counts confirming no race
conditions or data corruption. Speedup from 1 to 4 threads was
strong at 3.4x. Performance dropped at 8 threads due to thread
scheduling overhead and lock conflicts outweighing the benefit of
additional parallelism.

